                    hrs_unit:               `hrs`,
                    coach_header:           `Facilitator (the QA / accreditation panel)`,
                    coach_intro:            `You market managers “who can be held accountable for results”, yet your flagship course can't observe a single individual. Don't just relocate the failure — a high-stakes final exam raises validity and collapses completion; peer scores bolted onto heavy group work change nothing; a beautiful blueprint that needs 1,400 hours never ships. Design accountability in: visible early, supported to succeed, within 600 hours and R1.6M.`,

                    validity_label:         `Individual Validity`,
                    validity_sub:           `Does the assessment evidence each individual's learning? (0–100)`,
                    clarity_label:          `Accountability Clarity`,
                    clarity_sub:            `How visible is individual ownership during the term? (0–100)`,
                    completion_label:       `Completion`,
                    completion_sub:         `Projected cohort completion · baseline 92% (don't collapse it)`,
                    risk_label:             `QA Risk`,
                    risk_sub:               `Re-moderation / accreditation risk (lower is better)`,
                    budget_label:           `Marking & support hours committed`,

                    clock_r1:               `8 weeks to QA approval`,
                    clock_r2:               `Within ~600 marking hours/cohort`,
                    clock_r3:               `Make the individual visible in-term`,
                    clock_r4:               `Deploy R1.6M · face the panel`,

                    round_name_r1:          `Diagnose`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Redesign`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Checkpoints`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Support & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the accountability gap`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `External moderators flagged the flagship “Managing for Results” course: <strong>70% of the grade</strong> comes from one final group project, so they can't evidence what each individual learned. The headline numbers look fine — <strong>88% pass, 92% completion</strong> — but an anonymous survey shows <strong>41% report free-riding</strong>, and the peer-evaluation data you collect explains only <em>~8% of the variance</em>. Map each learning outcome to whether it is assessed at the individual or group level, and name the validity gap explicitly: this is a <em>constructive-alignment</em> failure — individual outcomes assessed through a group artefact.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Redesign the assessment`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Rebalance the blueprint to evidence individual learning <strong>without destroying collaboration</strong>. Weights must total 100% across an individual exam/viva, an individual applied/reflective component, weighted peer evaluation and a reduced-weight group project. Each choice trades validity, accountability-clarity, marking cost and completion risk — within the <strong>~600 marking-hour budget</strong>. The applied component gives the most validity per marking hour; the exam is costly; peer evaluation supplements but cannot substitute; cut the group below ~25% and you start to <em>destroy the very collaboration outcome the course exists to develop</em>.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Set accountability checkpoints`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Decide <strong>when and how</strong> an individual first becomes visibly accountable during the term — an early diagnostic, a mid-course individual checkpoint, contribution tracking, and a clear feedback/escalation path. An all-summative scheme means a student lost in <em>week 3 still discovers it in week 12</em>, hidden by the same group cover until then. Name who owns the early-alert trigger and who acts on it: accountability must be made visible early, not only at the final grade.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Allocate support & defend the design`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Deploy the <strong>ZAR 1.6M</strong> support budget to protect completion under the more demanding individual assessment, then defend the redesign to the QA / accreditation panel. The risk is concentrated in the <strong>bottom quartile and two weak competencies</strong> — spread the money evenly and you under-serve exactly the students the new assessment will expose. Defend that you raised rigour <em>and</em> protected completion within budget: lead with the outcome-to-assessment matrix, validity per marking hour, the checkpoint timeline and the targeted support plan.`,

                    r1_diagnosis_label:     `Where does individual accountability actually disappear in the current design?`,
                    r1_outcome_label:       `Which stated learning outcome is most damaged by group-only assessment?`,
                    r1_evidence_label:      `How hard do you push to name the validity gap explicitly in the diagnosis? (%)`,
                    r1_evidence_readout:    `Force of the explicit validity-gap finding:`,
                    diag_alignment:         `Constructive alignment fails — individual learning outcomes are assessed almost entirely through a single group artefact, so the grade can't evidence the individual`,
                    diag_passrate:          `Nowhere serious — an 88% pass rate and 92% completion show the course is working; the moderators are being pedantic`,
                    diag_peeronly:          `Only that we don't grade the peer-evaluation data we already collect — switch it on and the gap closes`,
                    diag_teaching:          `It's a teaching-quality problem, not an assessment one — better lectures will fix the spread`,
                    out_individual:         `“Holds an individual manager accountable for results” — the school's headline promise, yet never assessed at the individual level`,
                    out_teamwork:           `“Collaborates effectively in teams” — this is the one the group project actually evidences well`,
                    out_none:               `None is damaged — the group project covers every outcome adequately`,

                    r2_blueprint_label:     `Rebalance the assessment blueprint (weights must total 100%).`,
                    r2_total_readout:       `Total weight:`,
                    r2_total_cap:           `% / 100%`,
                    r2_blueprint_hint:      `A heavy individual exam/viva weight is the costliest to mark; the applied component gives the most validity per marking hour. Cut group below ~25% and you start to destroy the collaboration outcome the course exists to develop.`,
                    r2_workload_label:      `How do you keep the design within the ~600-hour marking budget?`,
                    alloc_exam:             `Individual exam / viva — high validity, high marking cost`,
                    alloc_applied:          `Individual applied / reflective component — strong validity per marking hour`,
                    alloc_peer:             `Weighted peer evaluation — supplements but cannot substitute (≈8% variance)`,
                    alloc_group:            `Group project (reduced weight) — keeps the collaboration outcome alive`,
                    work_optimize:          `Optimise validity per marking hour — lean on the applied component, use the viva on a sample, keep moderation sustainable`,
                    work_ignore:            `Ignore the constraint — individual vivas for all 280 students plus three written assessments; QA can find the hours`,
                    work_cutmod:            `Hit the budget by cutting moderation — skip cross-section re-moderation to save the hours`,

                    r3_checkpoint_label:    `When does an individual first become visibly accountable in the term?`,
                    r3_contribution_label:  `Do you track individual contribution within the group work?`,
                    r3_escalation_label:    `Who owns the early-alert trigger and acts on it?`,
                    chk_early:              `Week 3 early diagnostic + a mid-course individual checkpoint — problems surface while there's still time to act`,
                    chk_mid:                `A single mid-term individual test around week 7 — better than nothing, but late for the bottom quartile`,
                    chk_summative:          `All summative — everything counts at the end; a student lost in week 3 still discovers it in week 12`,
                    con_tracked:            `Yes — logged contribution + weighted peer evaluation surfaces free-riding while collaboration continues`,
                    con_peeronly:           `Peer scores only — students rate each other “excellent” to keep the peace, so the 41% free-riding signal is unchanged`,
                    con_none:               `No — contribution stays invisible inside the group, as it is today`,
                    esc_named:              `A named convenor owns it — a flagged student gets a feedback loop and a support referral within the week`,
                    esc_advisor:            `The student advisory office, end of term — they review flags after grades are in`,
                    esc_noone:              `No explicit owner — assume tutors will notice and raise it informally`,

                    r4_alloc_label:         `Allocate the ZAR 1.6M support budget (must total R1.6M).`,
                    r4_total_readout:       `Support budget allocated:`,
                    r4_total_cap:           `/ R1.6M`,
                    r4_defence_label:       `Defend the redesign to the QA / accreditation panel.`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that you raised rigour AND protected completion within budget? (%)`,
                    alloc_targeted:         `Targeted tutoring for the at-risk bottom quartile & two weak competencies`,
                    alloc_writing:          `Writing / quant support open to all students`,
                    alloc_alert:            `Early-alert system + convenor moderation time`,
                    def_evidence:           `Lead with evidence — show the outcome-to-assessment matrix, validity per marking hour, the checkpoint timeline and the at-risk support plan`,
                    def_rigour:             `Lead with rigour alone — “we raised the individual weighting”, and trust the panel won't ask about completion`,
                    def_passrate:           `Lead with the headline numbers — point at the old 88% pass rate and argue the course was never really broken`,

                    warn_evidence_range:    `The force of the validity-gap finding must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_weight_negative:   `Assessment weights cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_weight_total:      `The assessment weights must total exactly 100%. Re-balance the blueprint.`,
                    warn_support_negative:  `Support-budget allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_support_total:     `The support budget must total exactly R1.6M — the incremental envelope is fixed. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_alignment:     `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> This is a constructive-alignment failure: individual learning outcomes assessed almost entirely through one group artefact. The 88% pass rate is an artefact of group work, not evidence of individual competence — and that's exactly what the moderators can't sign.`,
                    coach_r1_peeronly:      `<strong>Switching on the peer scores won't close the gap.</strong> Peer evaluation explains only ~8% of the variance and your 41% free-riding signal is unchanged — students rate each other “excellent” to keep the peace. Peer data supplements direct individual assessment; it can't replace it.`,
                    coach_r1_passrate:      `<strong>“The numbers look healthy” is exactly the trap.</strong> An 88% pass rate masks a wide spread — the bottom quartile exits unable to perform the core competencies. The moderators called it an artefact of group work, not evidence of individual learning. Don't defend the symptom; name the validity gap.`,
                    coach_r1_teaching:      `<strong>Better lectures won't fix an assessment that can't observe the individual.</strong> This is a measurement failure, not a teaching one — the grade is built from a group artefact, so you cannot evidence what any one student learned no matter how good the teaching is.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the constructive-alignment failure AND the outcome it damages most — “holds an individual accountable for results”, the school's own headline promise, never assessed at the individual level. That is the exact gap the redesign has to close.`,
                    coach_r1_teamwork:      `<strong>Teamwork is the one outcome the group project does evidence well</strong> — that's not the damaged one. The outcome that disappears is individual accountability for results, the very thing Highveld markets. Don't fix what isn't broken and miss what is.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Nothing is damaged” is the moderators' point in reverse.</strong> The course can't show that any individual achieved the outcomes — a healthy headline pass rate is precisely what hides it. If nothing were damaged, the QA review wouldn't have flagged it.`,
                    coach_r1_softgap:       `<strong>A soft diagnosis won't survive the panel.</strong> If you don't name the validity gap explicitly — individual outcomes assessed through a group artefact — the redesign has no anchor and the moderators will ask what, exactly, you set out to fix.`,
                    coach_r2_peerbolt:      `<strong>You kept the group project heavy and bolted on peer scores.</strong> Peer evaluation explains ~8% of the variance and your free-riding signal is unchanged — the moderators still can't see individual learning. What did the peer scores actually evidence?`,
                    coach_r2_examheavy:     `<strong>You moved most of the grade onto a single final individual exam.</strong> Validity is up — but in the pilot, completion dropped from 92% to 74%, and the panel now asks why you traded one defect for another. Did you design accountability, or just relocate the failure? You'll need targeted support and early checkpoints to recover this.`,
                    coach_r2_killedgroup:   `<strong>You cut group work almost out to fix the validity gap.</strong> But “Managing for Results” is meant to develop collaboration AND individual accountability, and your alumni value the teamwork most. The goal is to make individual contribution visible WITHIN collaboration — not to abolish it.`,
                    coach_r2_groupheavy:    `<strong>Group weighting is still dominant.</strong> With the bulk of the grade riding on a shared artefact, the validity gap the moderators named is essentially unsolved. Shift weight to individual evidence — especially the applied component — without killing the group entirely.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A balanced blueprint that evidences the individual and keeps real collaboration.</strong> Individual weight up, the applied component carrying the load efficiently, the group project reduced but alive. That's constructive alignment — now make sure it fits the marking budget.`,
                    coach_r2_overbudget:    `<strong>Beautiful blueprint — and it needs about 1,400 marking hours against a 600-hour budget.</strong> Individual vivas for all 280 students plus three written assessments: faculty won't deliver it and QA won't approve an unsustainable design. Which assessment gives you the most validity per marking hour?`,
                    coach_r2_cutmod:        `<strong>You hit the hours by cutting moderation.</strong> But re-moderation across sections and convenors is the reliability the moderators care about most. Skip it and your redesign is valid on paper but inconsistent across the cohort — and that's the next thing they flag.`,
                    coach_r3_early:         `<strong>Early checkpoints are how accountability becomes visible in time.</strong> A week-3 diagnostic plus a mid-course individual checkpoint surfaces the struggling student while there's still term left to act — instead of discovering it at the final grade.`,
                    coach_r3_mid:           `<strong>One mid-term test is better than all-summative</strong> — but week 7 is late for a student already lost in week 3. The bottom quartile needs an earlier signal to give the support budget something to act on.`,
                    coach_r3_summative:     `<strong>Your redesign is all summative — everything counts at the end.</strong> A student who's lost in week 3 still discovers it in week 12, with the same group cover hiding it until then. Where in the term does an individual first become visibly accountable, and who acts on it?`,
                    coach_r3_peeronly:      `<strong>Peer scores alone don't surface free-riding.</strong> Students rate each other “excellent” to keep the peace, so your 41% signal is unchanged. Logged contribution plus weighted peer evaluation is what actually makes individual effort visible inside the group.`,
                    coach_r3_notracked:     `<strong>With no contribution tracking, the free-rider stays invisible</strong> — exactly the diffusion of accountability the survey caught. The group artefact hides who did what, and the moderators' original concern is untouched.`,
                    coach_r3_owned:         `<strong>Early checkpoints with a named owner who acts — that's a real accountability system.</strong> A flagged student gets a feedback loop and a support referral within the week, so the early signal turns into action rather than a report no one reads.`,
                    coach_r3_noowner:       `<strong>An alert with no owner is just data.</strong> If no named convenor acts on a flag within the week, the early signal dies in an inbox and the at-risk student discovers the problem at the final grade anyway.`,
                    coach_r4_targeted:      `<strong>Targeting the bottom quartile and the weak competencies is where the R1.6M earns its keep.</strong> The new assessment will expose exactly those students — concentrate the support where the risk is, not where it feels fair.`,
                    coach_r4_even:          `<strong>You spread the support budget roughly evenly.</strong> But the risk is concentrated in the bottom quartile and two specific competencies. Even support feels fair, yet it under-serves exactly the students the new assessment will expose. Where should the R1.6M actually go?`,
                    coach_r4_collapse:      `<strong>Your exam-heavy redesign already pushed completion toward the 74% pilot floor</strong> — and the support you've allocated isn't targeted enough to pull it back. Concentrate tutoring on the at-risk quartile and fund the early-alert path, or you ship a rigorous scheme that fails too many students.`,
                    coach_r4_evidence:      `<strong>That's the defence that holds.</strong> The outcome-to-assessment matrix, validity per marking hour, the checkpoint timeline and the targeted support plan answer the moderators' original concern directly — you raised rigour AND protected completion within budget. The NQF credit standing holds.`,
                    coach_r4_rigour:        `<strong>“We raised the individual weighting” is half a defence.</strong> The panel's whole point is the completion–rigour trade-off — if you can't show you protected completion within budget, raising rigour alone is just the exam-heavy error in a suit.`,
                    coach_r4_passrate:      `<strong>Pointing at the old 88% pass rate is defending the thing they flagged.</strong> That number is the artefact of group work the moderators rejected. Arguing the course was never broken tells the panel you didn't understand the finding — that's how the NQF credit goes “under review”.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
