                    insight_approved:       `Approved & accountable: you fixed the constructive-alignment failure as a system — a balanced blueprint that evidences the individual while keeping real collaboration, early checkpoints with a named owner, and targeted support that protected completion. The panel can sign it, and the NQF credit standing holds.`,
                    insight_collapse:       `Completion collapsed: you raised individual rigour — often via an exam-heavy grab — without the staged checkpoints and targeted support to carry the at-risk quartile. You traded one defect for another, exactly as the pilot warned (92% → 74%). Rigour without support shifts the failure; it doesn't solve it.`,
                    insight_gap:            `Validity gap unsolved: too much of the grade still rides on a shared group artefact, so the moderators still can't see individual learning. Peer scores and good intentions don't substitute for direct individual assessment — the original concern stands.`,
                    insight_killedgroup:    `Collaboration destroyed: you fixed the validity gap by cutting group work almost out of the course. But “Managing for Results” is meant to develop collaboration AND individual accountability — you fixed the measurement by destroying part of the learning outcome. The goal was to make individual contribution visible within collaboration, not to abolish it.`,
                    insight_short:          `A partial redesign. Some pieces are right, but validity, in-term visibility, completion protection and the panel defence don't yet add up to one coherent, sign-off-ready scheme.`,
                    insight_completion:     `Completion is below the line you must protect. The new assessment exposes the bottom quartile; without earlier checkpoints and support concentrated where the risk is, the more rigorous design pushes too many students out.`,
                    insight_balanced:       `You raised individual validity AND held completion near baseline — the completion–rigour trade-off managed, not ignored. That is the balance the QA review exists to test.`,
                    insight_overbudget:     `Your design exceeded the ~600 marking-hour budget. A scheme faculty can't sustain won't be approved no matter how valid it looks on paper — accountability design is an optimisation under a real resource constraint.`,
                    insight_invisible:      `Individual accountability is still barely visible during the term. Without early checkpoints and contribution tracking, a struggling student isn't seen until the final grade — the very pattern the redesign was meant to break.`,
                    insight_defended:       `Strong, defensible redesign: high validity, visible in-term accountability and low QA risk. You can answer the moderators' original concern with evidence rather than assertion — that is what protects the accreditation.`,
